From abe0fbe9d1c4b34bfc9508bd3c62c41a85cecebe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:53:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] PSCTRL: a host test for the settings surface

Runs on the build machine - no SDL, no libdrm, no JIT, no Pi:

    sh platforms/atari/psctrl/run-test.sh

It drives the descriptor table the way the accessory does and parses the
PS_DESCRIBE record with the SAME byte offsets psctrl.c uses, which is the
one place a silent disagreement between host and guest shows up as a
dialog full of nonsense rather than as a compile error. It also checks
that every enum's labels are present and non-empty (a missing one is a
radio with no caption, invisible on screen), that PS_GETINT on a settings
index agrees with DESCRIBE, that each class answers with its own result
code, that jit_power's two classes both work - 4 is applied live, 0 is
upgraded to deferred and turns the cache off only at the boundary, and it
comes back on - and that the .cfg writer keeps comments, keeps keys it
does not manage, writes each managed key exactly once, and leaves a .bak.

config_file_save.c is compiled with the C compiler on purpose: that is
how the real build compiles it, so the extern "C" linkage between it and
psctrl_settings.cpp is part of what this checks.

Two truncation fixes it prompted, both in paths that would only ever have
bitten on a very long floppy image name: the directory listing now skips
a name it could not hand back intact instead of silently cutting it, and
the insert path checks the join before making it.

Writing the test also caught a bug in the test: a 68k long is 32 bits, so
the shift-and-or in parse_desc() sign-extends m68k_speed's -1 ("max") all
by itself, while the same expression on a 64-bit host gives 4294967295.
The cast is what makes this the same test the accessory runs.
